WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.690
"The Batman" reviews are in – is the movie
another "Dark Knight?"

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.110
Or is it more of a lackluster night out?

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.700
"Oh, you're really not as smart as I thought
you were."

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.800
Matt Reeves' "The Batman" introduces us to
Robert Pattinson's version of the Dark Knight,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.230
who Thrillist has described as an "eerie take
on the Caped Crusader."

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.800
The Beat proclaims the new movie is the first
"Batman" incarnation "to pick up Nolan's mantle

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.619
where others have failed before."

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:32.149
At this point, the film has already scored
a "Certified Fresh" rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:37.090
Of course, with bold statements comparing
the latest iteration of Gotham's savior to

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:42.110
the one Nolan gave us in a trilogy, verdicts
from the other end of the spectrum were bound

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.120
to appear.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.090
Opinions are, after all, a lot like costumed
identities in Gotham City — everyone's got

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.230
to have one.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.420
But just what could possibly get stuck in
the cowl of critics who didn't see this new

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.090
movie as anything special?

00:00:55.090 --> 00:01:00.360
Well, by the sounds of things, it seems that
their issues have to do with similar traits

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.770
to the many, many Batman films that have come
before.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:09.880
In a 1.5 out of 5 star review for The Washington
Post, writer Ann Hornaday said,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.450
"'The Batman' [...] is a film that's as ponderous
as it is convoluted and, ultimately, devoid

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.689
of meaningful stakes."

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:22.689
She also describes it as, quote, "another
lugubrious, laboriously grim slog masquerading

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:24.350
as a fun comic book movie."

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:29.020
Naturally, considering the genre, the movie
is being compared to many superhero movies

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.750
that came before it – and for some reviewers,
it's coming up short.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.490
Blake Howard of Dark Horizons deemed that
"The Batman" strains to live up to what's

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:38.829
come before.

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:41.729
In the very first line of the review, Howard
writes,

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:47.470
"'The Batman' is a needless collage of not
only better movies, but more egregiously of

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:52.920
better Batman movies as director/co-writer
Matt Reeves and co-writer Peter Craig create

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.189
an utterly inferior and infuriating vision
of the Dark Knight."

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:00.709
"If this continues, it won't be long before
you've nothing left."

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:04.429
One element that seems to be getting a lot
of attention for all the wrong reasons is

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:08.349
the film's runtime, which brushes up against
three hours.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:13.200
This might not be an issue, had it not been
for the story, which to some felt overstuffed,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.960
even with all of that time to let things play
out.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.800
A review for Tilt Magazine described this
new take on the Bat as a, quote, "B-minus

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:28.040
serial killer thriller, one with a needlessly
convoluted plot, too many characters, unconvincing

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.030
twists, and a terrible villain."

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.420
That last point is particularly surprising,
considering that over reviews have singled

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.680
out Paul Dano's Riddler as a high point of
the movie.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.920
The New York Post's Johnny Oleksinski has
nothing but bad love for the Bat as well,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.140
making comparisons to the Nolan era of the
Caped Crusader's big screen outings.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.140
He wrote,

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.330
"Director Matt Reeves' downer movie embraces
the realism of 'The Dark Knight' — the opposite

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:57.960
of Tim Burton's purple-hazed funhouse — only
without the payoff of excellent writing and

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:58.960
acting."

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.220
While it's always been an aim for Batman to
strike fear into the hearts of criminals and

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.990
villains, this movie is eliciting impatience
from reviewers.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.400
As Oleksinski summed up,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.650
"There's an unshakable feeling here of 'What's
the point?'

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.310
Not to mention the nearly three-hour length.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.310
Holy runtime!"

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.510
"Selina – don't throw your life away."

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.260
For some reviewers, the movie's cast proved
incapable of elevating the material – including,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.440
in some writers' opinions, Robert Pattinson
as Batman.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.900
The Times' Kevin Maher wrote,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.650
"The performances are as complex as the colour
palette (ie not very).

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:38.350
Pattinson, who has delivered so many thrilling
turns recently, struggles to elevate his antihero

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.600
beyond a pouty emo brat."

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.280
Meanwhile, Moira McDonald with The Seattle
Times said that Pattinson " isn't given the

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.390
opportunity to bring much to the role."

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:53.270
Instead, his "young, eerily pale and very
intense Bruce Wayne" is another apparent entry

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:58.070
in what she describes as, quote, "the mercifully
sparse brotherhood of Superheroes Who Are

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.570
Too Moody to Wash Their Hair."

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:05.040
"Whoa, take it easy, sweetheart!"

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.569
Of course, other reviewers were effusive about
the movie; your mileage may vary.

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:13.930
Maybe it's worth seeing the film for yourself
when "The Batman" arrives in theaters on March

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:14.930
4th.

